SOLUTIONS = {'Maya had 245 stickers. She gave 87 stickers to a friend and then bought 3 packs of 12 more stickers. How many stickers does she have now?': 'Step 1: Subtract the stickers she gave away.\n  245 − 87 = 158\n\nStep 2: Find how many new stickers she bought.\n  3 packs × 12 = 36\n\nStep 3: Add the new stickers to what she had left.\n  158 + 36 = 194\n\nMaya has 194 stickers now.', 'A baker makes 6 trays of 24 muffins each. She sells 3 dozen muffins. How many muffins are left?': 'Step 1: Find the total muffins made.\n  6 trays × 24 muffins = 144 muffins\n\nStep 2: Find how many muffins were sold.\n  3 dozen = 3 × 12 = 36 muffins\n\nStep 3: Subtract to find how many are left.\n  144 − 36 = 108\n\n108 muffins are left.', 'Ethan read 18 pages a day for 5 days, then 25 pages a day for 3 more days. How many total pages did he read?': 'Step 1: Find pages read in the first 5 days.\n  18 × 5 = 90 pages\n\nStep 2: Find pages read in the next 3 days.\n  25 × 3 = 75 pages\n\nStep 3: Add both totals together.\n  90 + 75 = 165\n\nEthan read 165 pages in total.', 'A store had 500 pencils. It sold 45 pencils each day for 6 days. How many pencils are left?': 'Step 1: Find how many pencils were sold in 6 days.\n  45 × 6 = 270 pencils\n\nStep 2: Subtract from the starting amount.\n  500 − 270 = 230\n\n230 pencils are left.', 'Four friends split a $96 restaurant bill evenly. Each friend also leaves an extra $4 tip. How much does each person pay in total?': "Step 1: Find each person's share of the bill.\n  $96 ÷ 4 = $24\n\nStep 2: Add the $4 tip each person leaves.\n  $24 + $4 = $28\n\nEach person pays $28 in total.", "What's My Sign? Fill in the operations to make it true:\n20 ○ 4 ○ 6 = 11": 'Try each option:\n\nOption A (divide, then add):\n  20 ÷ 4 = 5\n  5 + 6 = 11 ✓\n\nAnswer: divide, then add (A).', "What's My Sign? Fill in the operations to make it true:\n9 ○ 6 ○ 3 = 51": 'Try each option:\n\nOption B (multiply, then subtract):\n  9 × 6 = 54\n  54 − 3 = 51 ✓\n\nAnswer: multiply, then subtract (B).', "What's My Sign? Fill in the operations to make it true:\n12 ○ 3 ○ 9 = 45": 'Try each option:\n\nOption A (multiply, then add):\n  12 × 3 = 36\n  36 + 9 = 45 ✓\n\nAnswer: multiply, then add (A).', "What's My Sign? Fill in the operations to make it true:\n50 ○ 14 ○ 4 = 9": 'Try each option:\n\nOption C (subtract, then divide):\n  50 − 14 = 36\n  36 ÷ 4 = 9 ✓\n\nAnswer: subtract, then divide (C).', "What's My Sign? Fill in the operations to make it true:\n36 ○ 6 ○ 5 = 30": 'Try each option:\n\nOption A (divide, then multiply):\n  36 ÷ 6 = 6\n  6 × 5 = 30 ✓\n\nAnswer: divide, then multiply (A).', 'What is 7,482 rounded to the nearest hundred?': "Step 1: Look at the tens digit in 7,482 — it's 8.\n\nStep 2: Since 8 is 5 or more, round the hundreds digit up.\n  7,482 → 7,500\n\n7,482 rounded to the nearest hundred is 7,500.", 'What is 3,256 rounded to the nearest thousand?': "Step 1: Look at the hundreds digit in 3,256 — it's 2.\n\nStep 2: Since 2 is less than 5, round down (keep the thousands digit the same).\n  3,256 → 3,000\n\n3,256 rounded to the nearest thousand is 3,000.", 'In the number 58,392, what digit is in the thousands place?': 'Step 1: Write out the place values of 58,392.\n  5 = ten-thousands\n  8 = thousands\n  3 = hundreds\n  9 = tens\n  2 = ones\n\nStep 2: The thousands digit is 8.\n\nAnswer: B (8).', 'Multiply: 234 × 6': 'Step 1: Break 234 into 200 + 30 + 4.\n\nStep 2: Multiply each part by 6.\n  200 × 6 = 1,200\n  30 × 6 = 180\n  4 × 6 = 24\n\nStep 3: Add the products.\n  1,200 + 180 + 24 = 1,404\n\n234 × 6 = 1,404.', 'Divide: 936 ÷ 8': 'Step 1: 8 × 100 = 800. Subtract: 936 − 800 = 136 left.\n\nStep 2: 8 × 17 = 136.\n\nStep 3: Add the parts together: 100 + 17 = 117.\n\n936 ÷ 8 = 117.', 'Which number is greatest?': 'Step 1: All four numbers start close to 45,000, so compare place by place.\n\nStep 2: 44,999 is the only one below 45,000 — eliminate D.\n\nStep 3: Compare the hundreds digit of the rest: 45,672 (6 hundreds), 45,067 (0 hundreds), 45,720 (7 hundreds).\n\nStep 4: 45,720 has the greatest hundreds digit.\n\nAnswer: C (45,720).', 'What is 4,000 + 300 + 20 + 5 written in standard form?': 'Step 1: Line up the place values: 4,000 (thousands) + 300 (hundreds) + 20 (tens) + 5 (ones).\n\nStep 2: Combine them: 4,325.\n\n4,000 + 300 + 20 + 5 = 4,325.', 'Round 68,459 to the nearest ten thousand.': "Step 1: Look at the thousands digit in 68,459 — it's 8.\n\nStep 2: Since 8 is 5 or more, round the ten-thousands digit up.\n  68,459 → 70,000\n\nAnswer: B (70,000).", 'Multiply: 47 × 32': 'Step 1: Break 32 into 30 + 2.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 47 × 30 = 1,410 and 47 × 2 = 94.\n\nStep 3: Add: 1,410 + 94 = 1,504.\n\n47 × 32 = 1,504.', 'A number has a 6 in the ten-thousands place, 3 in the thousands place, 0 in the hundreds place, 9 in the tens place, and 4 in the ones place. What is the number?': 'Step 1: Place each digit in its correct spot: 6 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nStep 2: Write them in order: 63,094.\n\nThe number is 63,094.', 'Which fraction is equivalent to 3/4?': 'Step 1: To find an equivalent fraction, multiply the numerator and denominator by the same number.\n\nStep 2: Multiply 3/4 by 2/2: (3×2)/(4×2) = 6/8.\n\nAnswer: A (6/8).', 'Which symbol makes this true?\n3/8 ○ 5/8': 'Step 1: The denominators are the same (8), so compare the numerators.\n\nStep 2: 3 is less than 5, so 3/8 is less than 5/8.\n\nAnswer: A (<).', 'What is 1/2 + 1/4?': 'Step 1: Rewrite 1/2 with a denominator of 4: 1/2 = 2/4.\n\nStep 2: Add: 2/4 + 1/4 = 3/4.\n\n1/2 + 1/4 = 3/4. Answer: B.', 'Which decimal is equivalent to 3/10?': 'Step 1: A fraction with denominator 10 converts directly to tenths in decimal form.\n\nStep 2: 3/10 = 0.3 (3 tenths).\n\nAnswer: A (0.3).', 'Which fraction is greater: 2/3 or 3/5?': "Step 1: Find a common denominator for 3 and 5 — that's 15.\n\nStep 2: Convert: 2/3 = 10/15 and 3/5 = 9/15.\n\nStep 3: Compare: 10/15 is greater than 9/15.\n\nAnswer: A (2/3).", 'What is 4/6 written in simplest form?': "Step 1: Find the greatest common factor of 4 and 6 — that's 2.\n\nStep 2: Divide both parts by 2: 4÷2=2, 6÷2=3.\n\n4/6 simplifies to 2/3. Answer: A.", 'Which decimal is equivalent to 7/10?': 'Step 1: A fraction with denominator 10 converts directly to tenths.\n\nStep 2: 7/10 = 0.7 (7 tenths).\n\nAnswer: C (0.7).', 'What is 5/6 − 1/6 written in simplest form?': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so subtract the numerators: 5 − 1 = 4, over 6 → 4/6.\n\nStep 2: Simplify: divide both parts by 2 → 2/3.\n\nAnswer: B (2/3).', 'A recipe uses 3/4 cup of sugar per batch. If Grace makes 4 batches, how many whole cups of sugar does she need?': 'Step 1: Multiply the amount per batch by the number of batches.\n  3/4 × 4 = 12/4\n\nStep 2: Simplify: 12/4 = 3.\n\nGrace needs 3 cups of sugar.', 'Of the 20 students in class, 1/4 brought their lunch. How many students brought their lunch?': 'Step 1: Finding 1/4 of a number means dividing by 4.\n  20 ÷ 4 = 5\n\n5 students brought their lunch.', 'A movie starts at 2:15 PM and ends at 4:05 PM. How many minutes long is the movie?': 'Step 1: From 2:15 PM to 4:15 PM is exactly 2 hours = 120 minutes.\n\nStep 2: The movie ends at 4:05, which is 10 minutes earlier than 4:15.\n\nStep 3: Subtract: 120 − 10 = 110.\n\nThe movie is 110 minutes long.', 'A rectangular garden is 12 feet long and 7 feet wide. What is its perimeter in feet?': 'Step 1: Perimeter of a rectangle = 2 × (length + width).\n\nStep 2: Add length and width: 12 + 7 = 19.\n\nStep 3: Multiply by 2: 19 × 2 = 38.\n\nThe perimeter is 38 feet.', 'A rectangular room is 9 feet by 6 feet. What is its area in square feet?': 'Step 1: Area of a rectangle = length × width.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 9 × 6 = 54.\n\nThe area is 54 square feet.', 'How many inches are in 3 feet?': 'Step 1: There are 12 inches in 1 foot.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 12 × 3 = 36.\n\nAnswer: C (36).', 'A recipe calls for 2 cups of flour. If Diego wants to triple the recipe, how many cups of flour does he need?': 'Step 1: Tripling means multiplying by 3.\n\nStep 2: 2 × 3 = 6.\n\nDiego needs 6 cups of flour.', 'How many minutes are in 2 and a half hours?': 'Step 1: 1 hour = 60 minutes, so 2 hours = 120 minutes.\n\nStep 2: Half an hour = 30 minutes.\n\nStep 3: Add: 120 + 30 = 150.\n\nAnswer: C (150).', 'Convert 5 pounds to ounces. (1 pound = 16 ounces)': 'Step 1: There are 16 ounces in 1 pound.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 5 × 16 = 80.\n\n5 pounds = 80 ounces.', 'Which unit would you use to measure the length of a football field?': "Step 1: Think about the size of a football field — it's about 100 yards long.\n\nStep 2: Inches would be far too small, and ounces/cups measure weight and volume, not length.\n\nAnswer: B (yards).", 'A train leaves the station at 9:40 AM and arrives at 11:05 AM. How many minutes was the trip?': 'Step 1: From 9:40 AM to 10:40 AM is exactly 1 hour = 60 minutes.\n\nStep 2: From 10:40 AM to 11:05 AM is 25 more minutes.\n\nStep 3: Add: 60 + 25 = 85.\n\nThe trip was 85 minutes long.', 'A pool is shaped like a rectangle, 25 meters long and 10 meters wide. What is the perimeter of the pool in meters?': 'Step 1: Perimeter of a rectangle = 2 × (length + width).\n\nStep 2: Add length and width: 25 + 10 = 35.\n\nStep 3: Multiply by 2: 35 × 2 = 70.\n\nThe perimeter is 70 meters.', 'Which term describes two lines that cross at a right angle?': "Step 1: Lines that cross to form a square corner (90°) have a special name.\n\nStep 2: That name is 'perpendicular.'\n\nAnswer: B (Perpendicular).", 'Which term describes two lines that never meet, no matter how far they extend?': "Step 1: Lines that stay the same distance apart forever and never touch are called 'parallel.'\n\nAnswer: B (Parallel).", 'An angle that measures exactly 90 degrees is called a(n) ___ angle.': 'Step 1: An angle of exactly 90° is called a right angle — like the corner of a piece of paper.\n\nAnswer: B (Right).', 'An angle that measures less than 90 degrees is called a(n) ___ angle.': "Step 1: An angle smaller than 90° (a 'sharp' angle) is called acute.\n\nAnswer: A (Acute).", 'An angle that measures more than 90 degrees but less than 180 degrees is called a(n) ___ angle.': "Step 1: An angle bigger than 90° but smaller than 180° (a 'wide' angle) is called obtuse.\n\nAnswer: C (Obtuse).", 'A shape with exactly 4 right angles and 4 equal sides is a ___.': "Step 1: A rectangle has 4 right angles but its sides aren't always equal.\n\nStep 2: A shape with 4 right angles AND 4 equal sides must be a square.\n\nAnswer: A (Square).", 'How many lines of symmetry does a square have?': 'Step 1: A square can be folded in half and match perfectly along 4 different lines: 2 diagonals and 2 lines through the middle of opposite sides.\n\nAnswer: B (4).', 'A triangle with all three sides of different lengths is called a(n) ___ triangle.': 'Step 1: A triangle where all 3 sides are different lengths is called scalene.\n\nAnswer: C (Scalene).', 'A triangle with two sides of equal length is called a(n) ___ triangle.': 'Step 1: A triangle with exactly 2 equal sides is called isosceles.\n\nAnswer: B (Isosceles).', 'Which figure has no straight sides and no angles?': 'Step 1: A circle is a curved shape — it has no straight sides and no corners (angles).\n\nAnswer: A (Circle).'}

def fetch_grade4_questions(subject):
    url = f"{BASE}/questions?grade=eq.4&subject=eq.{subject}&select=id,text&limit=500"
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={"apikey": KEY, "Authorization": f"Bearer {KEY}"})
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read())

def patch_solution(qid, solution):
    url = f"{BASE}/questions?id=eq.{qid}"
    data = json.dumps({"solution": solution}).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=data, method="PATCH", headers=HEADERS)
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as r:
        return r.status

print("Fetching grade 4 Math questions...")
questions = fetch_grade4_questions("Math")
print(f"Found {len(questions)} questions")

matched = 0
skipped = 0
for q in questions:
    text = q["text"]
    solution = SOLUTIONS.get(text)
    if solution:
        status = patch_solution(q["id"], solution)
        print(f"  \u2713 [{status}] {text[:60]}...")
        matched += 1
        time.sleep(0.1)
    else:
        skipped += 1

print(f"\nDone: {matched} updated, {skipped} not in this script's list")
